---
arrangement: whylog-corrupt-results-block-overruns
edit-loop: edit a sentence in the transcript below, then run `mise run loom:compile -- whylog-corrupt-results-block-overruns && mise run loom:promote -- whylog-corrupt-results-block-overruns`; prove it stuck with `mise run test`, which is also what catches anything else the promotion moved. `mise run loom:vars -- --all whylog-corrupt-results-block-overruns` lists this case's values; type {{name}} to insert or move one.
---
-- .whylog --
dorc-whylog/1 nonce=dorc attempt=0 host=fixture @@dorc@@
results bytes=9999

-- replay --
$ dorc why --last --whylog=.whylog
whylog: warning[whylog-corrupt]: the saved why-log is damaged and cannot be read
back (results block byte-count exceeds the file)
   = help:  this usually means an interrupted write; re-run `dorc why` live, and
            the next plan or apply will save a fresh why-log
